/// Callbacks of custom blocks, so a plugin can implement block logic.
///
/// A block calls back for none of its hooks until the plugin opts in with `set-block-callbacks`.
/// For every hook in the set the server fires an event that the plugin handles with
/// `context.register-event`. Handlers get the events of all blocks that opted in, tell
/// them apart with the `block` key. A block that did not opt in costs nothing: no event is made
/// and no handler runs. The `neighbor-update`, `update-shape`, `entity-collision` and `entity-step`
/// hooks run very often, handle them quickly.
///
/// The `state-index` of the events counts like the states of `block-registry`: add it to the
/// `base-state-id` of the block for the state id.
///
/// Redstone: a plugin machine usually knows its output from its own state. Set `redstone-output`
/// (and `comparator-output`) and tell the world with `world.set-block-redstone-output` whenever it
/// changes, that needs no event and updates the neighbours. The `weak-power`, `strong-power` and
/// `comparator-output` events instead ask the plugin at the moment redstone reads the block. To
/// read signals use `world.get-redstone-power` and its relatives. The events that return a result
/// count only from handlers registered as blocking.
///
/// Callbacks can be set until the server starts accepting connections (after plugin load and
/// `server-load-event`) and need the `registry.blocks` permission. Setting the same callbacks again
/// is fine, other callbacks for a block that has some fail.
interface block-callbacks {
    /// The hooks of a block to call the plugin for.
    flags block-callback-flags {
        /// A neighbour of the block changed: `custom-block-neighbor-update-event`.
        neighbor-update,
        /// The block was set in the world: `custom-block-placed-event`.
        placed,
        /// A player placed the block: `custom-block-placed-event` with the player set.
        player-placed,
        /// The block was replaced by another block or moved: `custom-block-state-replaced-event`.
        state-replaced,
        /// A player broke the block: `custom-block-broken-event`.
        broken,
        /// A tick scheduled with `world.schedule-block-tick` is due:
        /// `custom-block-scheduled-tick-event`.
        scheduled-tick,
        /// The block got a random tick: `custom-block-random-tick-event`.
        random-tick,
        /// An entity is inside the block: `custom-block-entity-collision-event`.
        entity-collision,
        /// An entity stands on or walks over the block: `custom-block-entity-step-event`.
        entity-step,
        /// The block is about to be placed: `custom-block-can-place-event`, which can refuse it.
        can-place,
        /// A neighbour changed and the block updates its shape: `custom-block-update-shape-event`,
        /// which can change the state of the block.
        update-shape,
        /// The block is a redstone signal source that uses the output stored with
        /// `world.set-block-redstone-output` (and its face variant). Redstone wire connects to it
        /// and repeaters, torches and neighbouring blocks read its power. Nothing is stored to
        /// start with, so the block gives no power until the plugin stores some. The output is
        /// kept in memory only and dropped when the block is replaced or moved: store it again
        /// after a restart. Without stored output this flag asks for no event, use `weak-power` and
        /// `strong-power` for that.
        redstone-output,
        /// Redstone readers ask for the weak power of the block with a
        /// `custom-block-redstone-power-event` whose `strong` is false, unless output is stored for
        /// the block. Makes the block a redstone signal source, like `redstone-output`. Runs
        /// whenever redstone looks at the block, handle it very quickly.
        weak-power,
        /// Redstone readers ask for the strong power of the block with a
        /// `custom-block-redstone-power-event` whose `strong` is true, unless output is stored for
        /// the block. Makes the block a redstone signal source, like `redstone-output`. Runs
        /// whenever redstone looks at the block, handle it very quickly.
        strong-power,
        /// Comparators can read the block: from the analog output stored with
        /// `world.set-block-comparator-output`, else with a `custom-block-comparator-output-event`.
        /// The output that is stored is kept in memory only and dropped when the block is replaced
        /// or moved. Placing the block updates the comparators next to it.
        comparator-output,
    }

    /// Sets the hooks of a block registered with `register-block` that call the plugin. Fails for
    /// an unknown block, other callbacks than before, or when registration is closed.
    set-block-callbacks: func(block-key: string, callbacks: block-callback-flags) -> result<_, string>;
}
